{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1160,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The moon orbits the earth once per month, \nwhich means the moon is on the sun side of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4520,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the earth every month. So... \"why aren't there \neclipses every month?\" is a question answered  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8520,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eloquently in a 1757 astronomy book by James \nFerguson. It's the 18th century equivalent of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13680,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"astronomy for dummies,\" complete with amazing \nillustrations. Here's Ferguson's surprisingly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18040,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good 250-year-old explanation for why there \naren't eclipses every month, illustrated by me. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23280,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"Every Planet and Satellite is illuminated by \nthe Sun; and casts a shadow towards that point  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27480,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Heavens which is opposite to the Sun.\nWhen the Sun's light is so intercepted by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31360,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Moon, that to any place of the Earth \nthe Sun appears partly or wholly covered,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34760,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he is said to undergo an Eclipse; though properly \nspeaking, 'tis only an Eclipse of that part of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth where the Moon's shadow falls. When the Sun \nis eclipsed to us, the Moon's Inhabitants on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "side next the Earth (if any such there be) see her \nshadow like a dark spot travelling over the Earth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48400,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about twice as fast as its equatoreal \nparts move, and the same way as they move. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52640,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the Moon's Orbit were coincident \nwith the Plane of the Ecliptic,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55600,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which the Earth always moves, the Moon's shadow \nwould fall upon the Earth at every New Moon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59800,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and eclipse the Sun to some parts of the Earth.\nBut one half of the Moon's Orbit is elevated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64200,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "5 degrees above the Ecliptic, and the other \nhalf as much depressed below it: consequently,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68640,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Moon's Orbit intersects the Ecliptic \nin two opposite points called the Moon's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72240,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nodes. When these points are in a line with \nthe Sun at New or Full Moon, the Sun, Moon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76520,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Earth are all in a line; and if the Moon \nbe then New, her shadow falls upon the Earth;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80960,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if Full the Earth's shadow falls upon her. \nWhen the Moon [is] more than 17 degrees from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85160,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "either of the Nodes at [a New Moon], the \nMoon is then too high or too low in her  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88760,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Orbit to cast any part of her shadow upon \nthe Earth. But when the Moon is less than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92200,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "17 degrees from either Node at the time of \nConjunction, her shadow falls upon the Earth. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "[The moon's] orbit contains 360 degrees; \nof which 17 [degrees], the limit of solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101040,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eclipses on either side of the Nodes, [is but \na small portion;] and as the Sun passes by the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105520,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nodes but twice in a year, it is no wonder that \nwe have so many New Moons without Eclipses.\""
    } ]
  } ]
}
